<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Git #2137 (Epic #1788 — Build Console → Build Queue Panel) — a GENUINELY
       separate WPF project for the Build Queue "Design Canvas" window that #2135
       first landed inside BuildConsole.csproj.

       Why this project exists (the real fix #2135 could not deliver): #2135 gave
       the window RUNTIME/style isolation (its XAML can't leak into the live
       BuildQueuePanel). It did NOT give it COMPILE/DEPLOY isolation — the window
       was still part of BuildConsole.csproj, so recompiling to iterate the design
       rebuilt the SAME BuildConsole.exe Shane's live, actively-running instance
       uses to manage his real dispatched builds (best case a file-lock error,
       worst case overwriting the binary under a running process).

       This project produces its OWN standalone BuildQueueDesignPreview.exe, in its
       OWN bin/ output directory, so Shane can compile and re-run the design window
       as many times as he wants with zero possibility of touching the live
       BuildConsole process.

       Share, don't duplicate: everything real is pulled from BuildConsole via the
       ProjectReference below —
         • the data-access layer (BuildQueuePostgresClient / QueueItem / the config
           + status-policy types the window reads), and
         • the #2126 color/typography TOKEN dictionaries (Themes/Colors.xaml +
           Typography.xaml), merged in App.xaml via a pack URI into the referenced
           assembly, exactly the two dicts #2135 shared by DynamicResource.
       No source is copied; the moved window (BuildQueueDesignWindow.xaml/.cs) is
       the only code that physically lives here.

       NOT wired as anyone's default startup — this stays a separate exe Shane
       launches deliberately (VS / `dotnet run` / the ActivityBar 🎨 button, which
       #2137 re-points to launch THIS exe). A deliberate cutover comes later.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <ApplicationIcon>..\BuildConsole\app.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <!-- Distinct assembly name → distinct exe, so it can never collide with or
         overwrite BuildConsole.exe. -->
    <AssemblyName>BuildQueueDesignPreview</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>BuildQueueDesignPreview</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Share BuildConsole's real code + the #2126 token dictionaries. This is a
         reference to a WinExe project; the SDK treats it as an assembly reference
         (BuildConsole's own entry point is never invoked from here — this project
         has its own App/Main). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\BuildConsole\BuildConsole.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Same app.ico as BuildConsole, linked (not copied) so the window's
         Icon="app.ico" resolves as a WPF Resource inside THIS assembly without
         duplicating the binary on disk. -->
    <Resource Include="..\BuildConsole\app.ico" Link="app.ico" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
